.TH VMLINUX 1
.SH NAME
vmlinux \- boot Linux as a guest
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vmlinux
[
.B -m
.I megabytes
] [
.B -a
.I append
] [
.B -i
.I initrd
] [
.B -s
.I seconds
] [
.B -t
.I line
] [
.B -q
.I pattern
] [
.B -d
.I seconds
] [
.B -p
.I 0000:bb:dd.f
] ... [
.B -b
.I file
] ... [
.B -n
.I etherdir
] ... [
.B -c
.I cpus
] ...
.I bzImage
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vmlinux
makes a machine in
.B /dev/vm
(see
.IR vm (3)),
loads a Linux
.I bzImage
by the 32-bit boot protocol \(em the kernel at 1 MiB,
.B boot_params
with the setup header, command line, initrd and an e820 map, a GDT for
CS 0x10 and DS 0x18 \(em starts it, and copies its console to standard
output and standard input to its console.
.B tsc_early_khz=
is added to the command line from the host's TSC rate.
.PP
.B -m
sets the memory (256 MiB);
.B -a
the command line
.RB ( console=ttyS0 );
.B -i
an initrd;
.B -p
assigns a PCI function, more than once for more;
.B -b
gives the guest
.I file
as a virtio disk (the first is its
.BR vda ),
and
.B -n
a virtio network interface on a bridge port of the
.IR ether (3)
device
.I etherdir
(such as
.BR /net/ether1 ),
each served by the kernel (see
.IR vm (3));
.B -c
gives it
.I cpus
processors, and ACPI tables (an RSDP, RSDT and MADT, in the BIOS area,
named in
.BR boot_params )
saying where they and the I/O APIC are;
.B -t
types
.I line
once the console shows the guest's shell marker
.RB ( "LINUXGUEST: shell" ),
which may be several lines (busybox's shell takes at most 1024
characters a line);
.B -q
stops the guest once the console shows
.I pattern
at the start of a line (so not in the echo of a typed command);
.B -s
stops it after
.I seconds
regardless;
.B -d
sets the machine's
.B trace
(see
.IR vm (3)).
The kernel and initrd are streamed into the machine's memory, not held.  An exit the kernel does not handle is reported with the
registers, and ends the run, as does a stop; the machine's status is
printed and the machine killed.
.PP
Nothing in it is local:
.B /dev/vm
may be a mount of another machine's, and the kernel and initrd are read
from wherever they are.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/vmlinux.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (3),
.IR vmsched (1)
